# SEGCORE app environment — MANAGED BY KOMODO. DO NOT EDIT ON THE HOST.
#
# Komodo writes this file to <repo path>/.env (mode 0600) on every Clone/Pull, right before it runs
# on_pull. Any edit made directly on the host is lost on the next deploy. To change a value, edit
# the Repo's Environment in the Komodo UI (Repos -> segcore-<host> -> Config -> Environment), or the
# corresponding Variable / secret. See docs/INSTRUCTIONS.md, "App .env management".
#
# NOTE: Komodo does not copy this file verbatim. It parses it into KEY=value pairs and re-serialises
# them, so **comments and blank lines are stripped** — everything below is documentation for whoever
# edits the template or the Repo environment, and never reaches the host. That is also why the
# warning is repeated as a real assignment (KOMODO_MANAGED_FILE): it is the only part of this header
# an operator looking at /opt/SEGCORE/.env will actually see. It is unused by the app.
#
# QUOTING — three consumers, three different rules, so the safe move is to keep metacharacters out
# of values entirely (generate secrets with `openssl rand -hex 32`, never a passphrase with & or
# spaces). What each consumer does:
#   Komodo writes the value verbatim. Quotes are not syntax to it: they end up in the file.
#   `./scripts/update.sh` SOURCES the file as shell, so an unquoted & | ; < > ( ) ` or space breaks
#     the deploy (DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASSWORD=M&Wnode&2000 gave "Wnode: command not found", 2026-08-21).
#     A value like that MUST be quoted.
#   docker compose strips surrounding quotes on ${VAR} interpolation, but `env_file:` does not — so a
#     quoted value reaches some containers with the quotes still attached.
# scripts/setup-app-env.sh lints for this, on the resolved values, before you deploy.
#
# Two values are always mangled and quoting does not save them: whitespace followed by # inside a
# value is treated as an inline comment and truncates it ("a #b" is written as "a), and trailing
# whitespace is trimmed. Both apply to interpolated values too, since Core substitutes before the
# file is parsed.
#
# Three kinds of placeholder appear below:
#
#   {{NAME}}   Rendered by the manager when this template seeds a Repo's Environment
#              (provisioning/server.py at onboarding, or scripts/setup-app-env.sh). Only MESH_IP
#              and HOSTNAME are derivable.
#
#   [[NAME]]   Interpolated at deploy time. Komodo Core resolves it from a Variable (Settings ->
#              Variables) or from the [secrets] block of the Core config file; whatever Core does
#              not know is resolved by that host's Periphery agent from the [secrets] block of
#              /etc/komodo/periphery.config.toml. Periphery-level secrets never cross the network
#              and are scoped to that single host — that is how a value is kept per-host.
#              An unknown name is NOT an error: it is written through literally. update.sh on the host
#              refuses to deploy when that happens.
#              CAUTION: a name that exists as a global Variable can NOT be overridden per host —
#              Core resolves first and the periphery never sees it. For a per-host value, the name
#              must exist ONLY in the periphery config.
#
#   CHANGE_ME  A per-host value nobody can derive. Filled in AFTER seeding, in the Komodo UI
#              (Repos -> segcore-<host> -> Config -> Environment) — deliberately not in the manager's
#              .env, so onboarding a host never means editing this repo. update.sh on the host aborts
#              the deploy while any CHANGE_ME is still there.
#
# SECRETS ARE FLEET-WIDE BY DESIGN. Every [[NAME]] below is a Komodo Variable shared by the whole
# segcore fleet, pushed from APPVAR_*/APPSECRET_* in the manager .env by scripts/setup-app-env.sh.
# The trade-off is deliberate: one place to manage each value, and the mesh ACL already denies
# app-host-to-app-host and app-host-to-manager traffic, so a credential taken from one host buys no
# access to another host's Postgres.
#
# A host that needs a different value is the EXCEPTION, and is handled in that Repo's Environment in
# the Komodo UI: replace [[NAME]] with the literal value, or with a host-specific Variable
# (JWT_SECRET=[[HOST1_JWT_SECRET]]) to keep it out of read/GetRepo, which returns literals in clear
# to anyone with read access. Do NOT try to override by putting the same name in the host's periphery
# [secrets]: Core interpolates first and the periphery never sees it.
#
# Two values are per host, both generated at onboarding into Komodo Variables <HOST>_JWT_SECRET and
# <HOST>_DB_PASSWORD (provisioning/server.py) — no manual step, and never regenerated.
#   JWT_SECRET   the app backends are PUBLIC, so a shared signing key would let a token minted on one
#                host authenticate as admin on every other tenant, over the internet.
#   DB_PASSWORD  it must match what that host's Postgres cluster was initialised with, so a
#                fleet-wide value could only ever be right by luck.
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY stays fleet-wide by decision: restoring one host's backup on another host is
# a wanted capability (failover drills, debugging), and that requires one key.
#
# Fleet-wide Variables this template expects (as APPVAR_*/APPSECRET_* in the manager .env):
#   MONITOR_DB_PASSWORD, DEFAULT_TENANT_CONFIG_KEY, DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASSWORD,
#   DEFAULT_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY, WEBHOOK_ALERTAS_TOKEN, SENTRY_DSN_BACKEND, VITE_SENTRY_DSN,
#   ALERTAS_EMAIL_TO, BACKUP_S3_BUCKET, BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY, BACKUP_S3_SECRET_KEY,
#   BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT, BACKUP_S3_REGION
# Plus, per host and created at onboarding: <HOST>_JWT_SECRET, <HOST>_DB_PASSWORD.

KOMODO_MANAGED_FILE=do-not-edit-here-edit-the-repo-environment-in-komodo

COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=segcore

# ================================================================
# SERVER
# ================================================================
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=production
BUILD_TARGET=production
TZ=Europe/Lisbon

# ================================================================
# NETWORK / DOMAINS
# ================================================================
# LOCAL_BIND_IP — where internal-only services bind (backend :3000, postgres-exporter :9187).
# This is the host's mesh IP; the manager scrapes these over the mesh, so it must not be 127.0.0.1.
LOCAL_BIND_IP={{MESH_IP}}
# Private domain for admin tooling, reachable over the mesh. Defaults to the mesh IP through nip.io,
# which resolves without any DNS setup and is valid on every host; override it in the Komodo UI on
# hosts that have a real internal DNS name (e.g. local.gims.pt).
LOCAL_BASE_DOMAIN={{MESH_IP}}.nip.io
# Public bind. Per host and not derivable: the host's own public IP. Bind the specific address rather
# than 0.0.0.0, the same rule the manager's Traefik follows — otherwise the public listener also
# answers on the mesh interface. Fill in in the Komodo UI.
PUBLIC_BIND_IP=CHANGE_ME

# ================================================================
# DATABASE (master)
# ================================================================
DB_HOST=postgres
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=postgres
# Per host, generated at onboarding into <HOST>_DB_PASSWORD. The password lives inside the Postgres
# cluster (fixed when it was initialised), not in this file: a fleet-wide value could only ever be
# right by luck, and being wrong locks the app out of its own database. Generating one per host makes
# that mismatch impossible. Postgres listens on the mesh IP, but the ACL denies app-host-to-app-host
# traffic, so this password is not a lateral-movement path between tenants either way.
DB_PASSWORD=[[{{HOST_UPPER}}_DB_PASSWORD]]
DB_MASTER_DATABASE=gims_master
# Read-only role for monitoring (pghero + postgres-exporter). MONITOR_DB_PASSWORD must match the
# manager's PGHERO_DB_PASSWORD — the manager keeps a single secret for the whole fleet, so this one
# is fleet-wide on purpose. See docs/pghero-host-setup.md.
MONITOR_USER=monitor
MONITOR_DB_PASSWORD=[[MONITOR_DB_PASSWORD]]

# ================================================================
# MULTITENANT
# ================================================================
MULTITENANT_ENABLED=true
VITE_MULTITENANT=true

# ================================================================
# DOCKER
# ================================================================
DOCKER_USER=1000
DOCKER_GROUP=1000

# ================================================================
# KEYS
# ================================================================
# JWT_SECRET signs the API tokens only — per host, generated once at onboarding into the Komodo
# Variable <HOST>_JWT_SECRET (provisioning/server.py) and never regenerated. Per host because the
# backends are public: a shared signing key would let a token minted on one host authenticate as
# admin on every other tenant. Rotating it only ends sessions (longest token is the 30d "remember
# me"); it destroys no data, PROVIDED TENANT_CONFIG_KEY below is set.
JWT_SECRET=[[{{HOST_UPPER}}_JWT_SECRET]]

# TENANT_CONFIG_KEY encrypts data AT REST — tenant config, integration credentials, SMTP credentials
# (backend/services/{TenantConfigService,TenantLocalConfigService,SmtpServerService}.js,
# services/integracoes/crypto.js). Fleet-wide on purpose: it has to travel with the data, so a
# backup from one host can be restored on another.
#
# ⚠️ Two rules. (1) The value must be the key the existing ciphertext was encrypted with — the app
# falls back to JWT_SECRET when this is unset, so on a host that never had it, that is that host's
# CURRENT JWT_SECRET. Setting anything else makes the stored credentials unreadable. (2) It must
# never go back to empty: the fallback would silently return to JWT_SECRET, which by then may have
# been rotated. update.sh on the host fails the deploy if it is empty.
TENANT_CONFIG_KEY=[[DEFAULT_TENANT_CONFIG_KEY]]

# ================================================================
# FILE STORAGE
# ================================================================
DEFAULT_STORAGE_PROVIDER=local
UPLOAD_PATH=/storage/uploads
MAX_FILE_SIZE=10485760

# ================================================================
# ADMIN default credentials
# ================================================================
# Grafana is no longer part of SEGCORE — GF_ADMIN_USER / GF_ADMIN_PASSWORD are deliberately absent.
DEFAULT_ADMIN_USER=admin
DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASSWORD=[[DEFAULT_ADMIN_PASSWORD]]

# ================================================================
# SENTRY
# ================================================================
SENTRY_DSN_BACKEND=[[SENTRY_DSN_BACKEND]]
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=[[VITE_SENTRY_DSN]]

# ================================================================
# BACKUPS (per host — each host backs up its own cluster; do not centralise)
# ================================================================
BACKUP_PATH=/storage/backups
BACKUP_ENABLED=true
BACKUP_CRON_SCHEDULE=0 3 * * *
BACKUP_MAX_CONCURRENT=1
# AES-256 key for the manual DR backups (scripts/backup.sh). IRRECOVERABLE if lost — keep a copy
# out-of-band, exactly like the manager's BACKUP_MASTER_KEY.
BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=[[DEFAULT_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY]]

BACKUP_S3_ENABLED=true
BACKUP_S3_PROVIDER=hetzner
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=[[BACKUP_S3_BUCKET]]
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY=[[BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY]]
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_KEY=[[BACKUP_S3_SECRET_KEY]]
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=[[BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT]]
BACKUP_S3_REGION=[[BACKUP_S3_REGION]]
BACKUP_S3_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ================================================================
# OTHER
# ================================================================
SWAGGER_ENABLED=false

# ================================================================
# SYSTEM ALERTS (app -> its own backend webhook)
# ================================================================
ALERTAS_EMAIL_TO=[[ALERTAS_EMAIL_TO]]
WEBHOOK_ALERTAS_TOKEN=[[WEBHOOK_ALERTAS_TOKEN]]
